               "opportunityCheck":  "Most sub-scores look reasonable. I would raise AI revenue exposure by 1–2 points: data center was 79% of Q2 FY27 revenue and grew 46%, which is more than a 15 suggests. I would leave bottleneck \u0026 pricing power at 11 for now. Lumentum\u0027s lasers are a real supply chokepoint, but the deal does not close until the first half of 2028 and could still fail on shareholder or regulatory votes. Market growth at 17 deserves a closer look if Stifel\u0027s 2028–29 capex-slowdown view gains support.",
               "technical":  "MRVL closed at $122.28 on 20 Nov. It opened at $132.89 and finished near the day\u0027s low of $120.56, which is a weak close. Trend: price is far below the EMA 50 ($199.66), EMA 100 ($207.39) and EMA 200 ($189.36). EMAs are moving averages that weight recent prices more. The EMA 50 has dropped below the EMA 100, an early sign the medium-term trend has turned down. Momentum: MACD (a trend-momentum gauge) is -25.23, below its signal line at -23.66, and the histogram is negative (-1.57), so selling pressure is still building. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 27.9. That means the selling is stretched, but it is not a reversal signal by itself. Volatility: the Bollinger Bands (a range of about two standard deviations around the 20-day average) run from $97.51 to $244.60, very wide after weeks of big swings. Price is in the lower half, below the $171.05 middle line. In the 52-week range of $70.69–$329.88, price sits near the bottom fifth, about 63% below the high. Volume of 40.3M was about 1.23x the 20-day average, so heavy trading backed the drop. Support levels are $120.56 and then $97.51. Resistance levels are about $133.55 (the 5 Nov close) and $138.47 (the 19 Nov close).",
               "fundamental":  "Marvell sits at two choke points in AI data centers: custom accelerators (XPUs) for cloud companies, and the optical DSPs that link AI servers. Q2 FY27 (quarter ended 1 Aug 2026): revenue was $2.739B, up 37% YoY. Data center revenue was $2.17B, up 46%. Non-GAAP gross margin was 58.9%. Q3 guidance is $3.15B (±2% after the 2 Nov update) with non-GAAP EPS of $1.10 ±$0.05. Management expects about $12B for FY27, and Stifel cites a roughly $20B outlook for FY28. Buying Lumentum would add lasers, transceivers and optical manufacturing, moving Marvell from chips into full optical modules, a market the companies size at $550B. That fits the optical-DSP moat. The cost is roughly 50% more shares, a close not expected until the first half of 2028, and integration risk.",
               "news":  [
                            "2 Nov 2026: Marvell agreed to buy Lumentum for stock only. Lumentum holders get 4.5 MRVL shares per share (about $115B EV, a 23.9% premium) and Marvell holders keep about 67%. Closing is expected in the first half of 2028. MRVL fell 12.7% that day, after a 13.2% drop on 29 Oct when Bloomberg reported the talks.",
                            "2 Nov 2026: In the same release, Marvell said preliminary fiscal Q3 revenue matched guidance. It kept the $3.15B midpoint and narrowed the range to ±2% from ±5%.",
                            "Early Nov 2026: BofA downgraded MRVL to Neutral and cut its target to $225 from $400. TD Cowen downgraded to Hold with a $175 target, and KeyBanc moved to Sector Weight. All cited dilution and deal timing.",
                            "5 Nov 2026: MRVL fell 16.4% with no company news, as the Nasdaq dropped 5.5% in an AI selloff. Some reports linked the selloff to newly elected governors promising to pause data-center construction.",
                            "16 Nov 2026: MRVL fell 7.3% as 33 of 34 large chip stocks declined ahead of Nvidia\u0027s earnings report.",
                            "20 Nov 2026: Stifel kept its Buy rating but cut its target to $280 from $370, citing slower 2028–29 capex and lower peer valuations. MRVL fell 11.69% in a broad tech selloff. Q3 FY27 results are expected around 1 Dec 2026."
